CH = dict(num=14, tag="Political Science · Ch 2", theme="Federal division of power, Indian federalism, language policy and decentralisation",
 title="Federalism",
 intro="Federalism is vertical power sharing between a central authority and constituent units. This chapter explains what federalism means, how India's federation works, the role of linguistic states and the language policy, centre-state relations, and how power is further shared with panchayats and municipalities.",
 big_questions=["What is federalism and what are its features?", "How is federalism practised in India?", "Why was decentralisation needed and how does local government work?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "What Is Federalism?"),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "**Federalism** is a system of government in which power is divided between a central authority and various constituent units of the country. Usually, a federation has two levels of government: one for the entire country, and the other at the level of provinces or states."),
 ("table", ["", "Unitary system", "Federal system"], [["Levels", "One level or sub-units subordinate to the centre", "Two or more levels with their own powers"], ["Central government", "Can pass orders to provincial government", "Cannot order the state governments to do something; states have power of their own"], ["Example", "Sri Lanka (for practical purposes a unitary system)", "India, USA, Switzerland, Belgium"]]),
 ("h3", "Key features"),
 ("ol", ["There are **two or more levels** of government.", "Different tiers govern the same citizens, but each has its **own jurisdiction** in specific matters of legislation, taxation and administration.", "The jurisdictions of the respective levels are **specified in the Constitution**, so the existence and authority of each tier is constitutionally guaranteed.", "The fundamental provisions of the Constitution cannot be unilaterally changed by one level of government; such changes require the consent of both levels.", "**Courts** have the power to interpret the Constitution and the powers of different levels of government; the **Supreme Court** acts as an umpire.", "**Sources of revenue** for each level are clearly specified to ensure its financial autonomy.", "Federations have a dual objective: to safeguard and promote **unity of the country**, while at the same time accommodating regional diversity."]),
 ("table", ["Type", "How it arises", "Examples"], [["**Coming together** federation", "Independent states come together on their own to form a bigger unit and retain identity; equal powers to states", "USA, Switzerland, Australia"], ["**Holding together** federation", "A large country decides to divide its power between the national government and states; the central government tends to be more powerful", "India, Spain, Belgium"]]),
 ("h2", "What Makes India a Federal Country?"),
 ("ul", ["The Constitution originally provided for a two-tier system of government: the Union government (central) and the State governments. A third tier of federalism was added later through **panchayats and municipalities**. India now has **28 states and 8 Union Territories** (as of the changes of 2019-2020).", "Constitution has clearly provided a **three-fold distribution** of legislative powers between the Union and State governments."]),
 ("table", ["List", "Subjects", "Who legislates"], [["**Union List**", "Subjects of national importance: defence of the country, foreign affairs, banking, communications and currency; need uniform policy", "Union Government alone"], ["**State List**", "Subjects of state and local importance: police, trade, commerce, agriculture and irrigation", "State Governments alone"], ["**Concurrent List**", "Subjects of common interest to both: education, forest, trade unions, marriage, adoption and succession", "Both; if their laws conflict, the **Union law prevails**"]]),
 ("p", "Subjects that do not fall in any list (**residuary subjects**, such as computer software that came after the Constitution) fall within the Union's jurisdiction. Not all units enjoy equal status: some units, such as **Delhi** and **Chandigarh**, are Union Territories, and the Union government has special powers in running them. Jammu and Kashmir and Ladakh became Union Territories in 2019. Sharing of power can be changed only if both Houses of Parliament pass it by two-thirds majority and half of the state legislatures approve."),
 ("h3", "How is federalism practised?"),
 ("ul", ["**Linguistic states:** The creation of linguistic states was the first and a major test for democratic politics in India. After 1947 the boundaries of several old states were changed so that people who spoke the same language lived in the same state (for example Andhra Pradesh and Karnataka, with **Maharashtra and Gujarat** formed in 1960). Some leaders feared this would split the country, but experience showed that linguistic states made India **more united** and made administration easier. Some states were created on the basis of culture, ethnicity or geography, such as **Nagaland** (1963), **Uttarakhand** and **Jharkhand** (both 2000).", "**Language policy:** The Constitution did not give the status of national language to any one language. **Hindi** was identified as the **official language** (about 40 per cent speak Hindi as mother tongue); there are **22 Scheduled Languages**; **English** is used with Hindi for official purposes. Central leaders were careful in not imposing Hindi on non-Hindi states; the Tamil Nadu agitation against Hindi is an example. Sri Lanka's treatment of Tamil is a contrast.", "**Centre-state relations:** Earlier, with the Congress ruling at the Centre and most states, state governments were not autonomous. After 1990, the rise of regional political parties and **coalition governments** at the Centre meant that the ruling party at the Centre had to share power and respect the autonomy of state governments; the Supreme Court's **S. R. Bommai judgement (1994)** made it difficult for the Centre to dismiss state governments arbitrarily."]),
 ("h2", "Decentralisation in India"),
 ("p", "When power is taken away from Central and State governments and given to **local government**, it is called **decentralisation**. The basic idea behind decentralisation is that there are a large number of problems and issues which are best settled at the local level, and people have better knowledge of problems in their localities."),
 ("ul", ["In **1992**, the Constitution was amended (**73rd and 74th Amendments**) to make the third-tier of democracy more powerful and effective: elections to local bodies are held regularly; seats are reserved for **SCs, STs and OBCs**; at least **one-third of all positions** are reserved for **women**; an independent institution called the **State Election Commission** conducts panchayat and municipal elections; State governments are required to share some powers and revenue with local bodies.", "**Rural:** The **Gram Sabha** is the body of all the voters in the village. **Gram Panchayat** has a president (**sarpanch**) and ward members (**panch**). A few panchayats together form a **Panchayat Samiti** (block or mandal); all the Panchayat Samitis in a district together constitute the **Zilla Parishad**.", "**Urban:** **Municipalities** in towns and **Municipal Corporations** in big cities, headed by a **Mayor** in a corporation and a **Municipal Chairperson** in a municipality.", "Local government increases people's participation, so that people's voice is heard; yet many state governments have not transferred sufficient powers and funds."]),
 ("h2", "Quick Revision: Likely Exam Questions"),
 ("qa", [
  ("Define federalism.", "System dividing power between a central authority and constituent units."),
  ("Differentiate coming together and holding together federations.", "Coming together: independent states join (USA); holding together: a large country divides power (India)."),
  ("Name the three lists of subjects.", "Union, State and Concurrent."),
  ("Who has power over residuary subjects?", "The Union Government."),
  ("Which languages are official?", "Hindi is official language; there are 22 scheduled languages; English is also used."),
  ("What is decentralisation?", "Sharing power with local governments."),
  ("What changes did the 73rd and 74th amendments make?", "Regular local elections, reservation including one-third for women, State Election Commission."),
 ]),
 ],
 takeaways=["Federalism divides power between **two or more levels** of government, with courts as umpire.", "Federations are **coming together** (USA) or **holding together** (India).", "Powers are distributed through **Union, State and Concurrent lists**; residuary powers rest with the Union.", "**Linguistic states** and a flexible **language policy** helped India's federal unity.", "**Panchayats and municipalities** were made the third tier by the 73rd and 74th Amendments (1992)."])
